findings.body:
`index.html` points the favicon links at `/ico/favicon-16x16.png` and the other files in `public/ico/` (the files really are in that folder). The cleanup writes back `/favicon-16x16.png`, `/favicon-32x32.png` and the matching `-dark` paths, without the `/ico/` prefix:

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/hooks/use-state-favicon.ts" lines="55-64"}

::callout{type="warning"}
After a user leaves any task run details page, the `favicon-16` and `favicon-32` links point at missing files for the rest of the session. The favicon breaks or the browser falls back to something else.

The cleanup also runs on every `stateType` change, but the next effect overwrites it right away, so you only see this after unmount.

A sturdier fix is to save the original `href` before changing it and restore that saved value, so the hook can't drift out of sync with `index.html`:

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/hooks/use-state-favicon.ts" lines="49-64"}
		const original16 = favicon16?.getAttribute("href");
		const original32 = favicon32?.getAttribute("href");
		if (stateType) {
			const faviconPath = `/${stateType.toLowerCase()}.svg`;
			favicon16?.setAttribute("href", faviconPath);
			favicon32?.setAttribute("href", faviconPath);
		}

		return () => {
			if (original16) favicon16?.setAttribute("href", original16);
			if (original32) favicon32?.setAttribute("href", original32);
		};

The hook builds `/${stateType.toLowerCase()}.svg` for any state type, but only seven SVGs were added. A paused or cancelling task run sets the favicon to `/paused.svg` or `/cancelling.svg`. Both return 404, so the tab shows a broken or blank icon.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/hooks/use-state-favicon.ts" lines="49-53"}

Either add `paused.svg` and `cancelling.svg`, or map those states to existing icons (e.g. CANCELLING → `cancelled.svg`, PAUSED → `scheduled.svg`), or leave the favicon alone for states with no icon. A small lookup map would make the supported set explicit:

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/hooks/use-state-favicon.ts" lines="49-53"}
		const icon = stateType ? STATE_FAVICONS[stateType] : undefined;
		if (icon) {
			favicon16?.setAttribute("href", icon);
			favicon32?.setAttribute("href", icon);
		}

The old code checked `!== null && !== undefined` explicitly, so a jitter factor of `0` showed as "0". The new code uses a truthy check, so `0` (a valid value meaning "no jitter") now shows "None":

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/task-runs/task-run-details/task-run-details.tsx" lines="176-183"}

That also doesn't match the neighbouring fields. Retries uses `??`, and Duration and Estimated Run Time use explicit null checks.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/task-runs/task-run-details/task-run-details.tsx" lines="179-181"}
					{taskRun.empirical_policy?.retry_jitter_factor != null
						? taskRun.empirical_policy.retry_jitter_factor.toString()
						: "None"}

The description says 10 test cases were added for `useStateFavicon`, but the diff has no `use-state-favicon.test.ts`. `ui-v2/src/hooks/` only contains `use-state-favicon.ts`. The sidebar now always renders every field with a "None" fallback, but `task-run-details.test.tsx` wasn't changed to check it.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/hooks/use-state-favicon.ts" lines="37-66"}

A test that checks the href after unmount would have caught the missing `/ico/` prefix above. A sidebar test with `retry_jitter_factor: 0` would have caught the jitter regression. `use-page-title.test.ts` is a pattern to follow.

The condition went from `flow_run_name && flow_run_id` to just `flow_run_id`. If the name hasn't loaded or is null, the row shows only an external-link icon with no text.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/task-runs/task-run-details/task-run-details.tsx" lines="45-57"}

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/task-runs/task-run-details/task-run-details.tsx" lines="55-55"}
							{taskRun.flow_run_name ?? taskRun.flow_run_id}

The favicon `<link>` elements in `index.html` have `media="(prefers-color-scheme:light)"` and `media="(prefers-color-scheme:dark)"`. When `getPreferredColorScheme()` returns `no-preference`, the hook updates the light links, but their media query doesn't match, so nothing visibly changes. The color scheme is also read once per effect run, so switching OS theme while on the page leaves the newly active links unchanged.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/hooks/use-state-favicon.ts" lines="39-47"}

A simpler approach is to update all four links (light and dark) every time and let their `media` attributes decide which one applies. That removes the `matchMedia` branching completely.
